latforms":["Linux"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"parameters":[{"name":"terminals","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The vertices to connect.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"name":"weight","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each edge’s weight, from its position.","type":"text"}]}]}],"kind":"parameters"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Return Value","level":2,"anchor":"return-value"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The tree’s edges, in position order, and their total weight."}]}],"kind":"content"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Discussion","level":2,"anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"One Dijkstra search from all terminals at once assigns every vertex to its nearest","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"terminal. Each edge between two terminals’ regions joins them at the length of the path","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"through it, a minimum spanning tree of those joins picks which regions to connect, and the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"tree is the picked edges with the shortest paths from their ends to their terminals. Its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"weight is at most 2(1 − 1\/ℓ) times the optimum, where ℓ is the number of leaves of an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"optimal tree. Every leaf is a terminal.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Repeated terminals count once. With fewer than two terminals the tree has no edges. Every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge that is not a self-loop is weighed once; ","type":"text"},{"code":"weight","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is not kept.","type":"text"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"O(m log m).","type":"text"}]}],"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Complexity"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every terminal is a 